      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Range of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5).$",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(4x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -2,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$",
