      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Domain and Asymptotes of a Transformed Function",
      "problem": "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+{30}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x+{30}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx+{30}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+{30}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
